<TopicPage slug="subjects/landscape-architecture/foundational/landscape-design-process">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Landscape Architecture, and it assumes nothing — no drawing
    skill, no botany, no construction knowledge. What it asks for is a shift of habit: learning
    to read a piece of ground the way a designer reads it, before imagining anything on it.
    Everything else in the subject builds on the process taught here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>run a site inventory and turn it into an analysis — facts on one side, design consequences on the other;</li>
    <li>follow the professional process from research and mapping through concept, design development, documents and stewardship;</li>
    <li>use the studio's core tools: functional diagrams, the parti, grading arithmetic and layered planting design;</li>
    <li>place any garden or park you meet inside the history of landscape design — formal, English, Japanese, public-park, modern and ecological traditions — and say what each contributed;</li>
    <li>explain why a landscape design is never "finished" at construction, and what stewardship therefore owes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: in this discipline the site is not a blank canvas but a co-author.
    We read first and draw second — always in that order.
  </p>

  <h2>Design with a living medium</h2>
  <p>
    Plant two trees of the same species the same day, and within ten years they will be two
    different plants — one thriving, one struggling — because no two sites are identical. That
    simple observation separates landscape architecture from its neighbouring disciplines.
    A building is designed, built and finished; a landscape grows, moves, dies back and matures
    on decadal timescales, changes by season and by year, and is never complete the way a
    completed building is. Those facts do not merely complicate the work — they bend the whole
    discipline's process.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the medium is alive, the process begins not with sketching but with
    <em>reading</em>: the site's ecology, hydrology, soils, microclimate and cultural history.
    It proceeds through diagrams before drawings and concepts before details. And it ends years
    after construction, when the planted idea finally stands up and the maintenance regime the
    designer planned — or failed to plan — reveals itself. In operations-management terms this
    is a <em>project</em> process, the hardest kind to manage: a one-time event, highly
    customised, resource-hungry, with no prototype and no second attempt. A landscape design
    firm never builds the same garden twice, which is exactly why the profession standardises
    the <em>process</em> instead of the product.
  </p>
  <p>
    The professional process, taught in every accredited programme and codified in the
    accreditation standards, runs: research the site, analyse and map it, generate a concept,
    develop the design, and deliver documents — then steward the result. Around that sequence
    sits a body of theory — a history of how the best designers have arranged land, water,
    plants and people. This lesson covers the three foundations: how to see a site, how designs
    are generated in the studio, and where the discipline's design ideas come from.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the design process, site-first" widget="equationStep" config={processCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the order is the argument. Research and analysis come
    before any concept — step 2 feeds step 3, not the reverse. Notice too that the list is not
    a one-way street in spirit: a concept that fails against the analysis is sent back, and
    every later step (development, documents, stewardship) can expose something the analysis
    missed. The sequence is a loop that tightens; the arrows only point forward on paper.
  </p>

  <h2>Site analysis and inventory: the site is the brief</h2>
  <p>
    Two designers can walk the same lot and see different projects, but neither should see
    nothing — and the difference between seeing and merely looking is method. The profession
    splits the first phase into <strong>inventory</strong> (collecting facts) and
    <strong>analysis</strong> (interpreting them into design consequences). Keep the two words
    separate: an inventory says "water pools at the back corner after heavy rain"; an analysis
    says "therefore either regrade the corner or make it a feature". Facts first, consequences
    second, in that order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The inventory is systematic. <em>Climate and sun</em> — the sun's path across the seasons (a
    solar-geometry problem with real stakes: summer shade is wanted where winter light is
    precious), prevailing winds, frost pockets. <em>Water</em> — where rain arrives, flows,
    pools and exits, at what rates. <em>Soil</em> — profiles, drainage, compaction, bearing
    capacity, pH. <em>Vegetation</em> — existing trees, which are assets to be kept, with their
    root zones protected. <em>Views</em> — what deserves framing, what deserves screening.
    <em>Circulation</em> — how people (and water, and wildlife) actually move, which is often
    not how the plan assumes. <em>Regulation and utilities</em> — zoning, easements, buried
    lines. <em>Culture</em> — the site's history and the neighbourhood's habits. None of this is
    decoration for a report; every item is a constraint or an opportunity with a construction
    consequence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The analysis phase then overlays the findings — historically on translucent sheets, today in
    GIS layers — and the discipline's signature method appears: <strong>suitability
    analysis</strong>, Ian McHarg's layered-map technique from <em>Design with Nature</em>
    (1969), in which each factor (slope, soil, drainage, habitat) is mapped and the overlays
    reveal where building, planting and play belong. What distinguishes professional analysis
    from decoration is that it generates the design rather than merely preceding it. A chronic
    drainage problem becomes a <strong>rain garden</strong> — the nuisance re-cast as a feature
    that manages stormwater on site. A blazing west-facing wall becomes a <strong>deciduous
    shade strategy</strong> — a tree that screens summer sun and drops its canopy to admit
    winter light. A noisy road edge becomes a berm-and-planting sound design. Good analysis, the
    studio maxim says, usually contains the concept; the designer's first job is to listen until
    it speaks.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that landscape design is plant selection — picking pretty
  species for pretty beds. Planting is one deliverable among many (grading, drainage, paving,
  structures, lighting), and it is decided <em>last</em>, inside a spatial and functional
  structure. The reverse order — shopping for plants, then finding somewhere to put them — is
  the amateur signature, and it fails in the first summer drought.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the process starts with listening. Inventory collects facts,
    analysis converts them into consequences, and a well-made analysis often already contains
    the concept. Now the studio turns that concept into a design.
  </p>

  <h2>Design generation and the studio: from diagram to construction</h2>
  <p>
    Landscape design is learned, almost universally, in the <strong>studio</strong> — the
    drafting-room classroom where students work through full projects under repeated critique.
    The studio's method is a process and a pedagogy at once. <strong>Programming</strong> states
    what the design must achieve: uses, areas, adjacencies, budget. <strong>Concept
    generation</strong> then begins with <strong>functional diagrams</strong> — bubble diagrams
    in which spaces are circles and relationships are arrows (kitchen near terrace, play visible
    from kitchen, service hidden). The reason for bubbles is economic: it is far cheaper to move
    a circle than a wall. From the bubbles condenses a <strong>parti</strong> — the organising
    idea of the whole composition, one diagram that explains the design: "a spine path threading
    three garden rooms", "the slope terraced into sequential outdoor rooms". A strong parti
    survives the budget cuts and value engineering that will certainly come; a weak one
    dissolves into decoration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Design development</strong> then gives the concept its body, and here the living
    medium bites. <em>Grading</em> — shaping the ground itself — is the field's most
    under-appreciated art: contours are manipulated to drain water, level a terrace, screen a
    road and create enclosure, with cut-and-fill balanced because trucking soil is money.
    <em>Planting design</em> composes in layers — canopy, understory, shrub, perennial,
    groundcover — each chosen for function (shade, screening, seasonal interest, habitat,
    stormwater uptake) as well as appearance, and each placed by mature dimensions.
    <em>Materials</em> — paving, walls, edges, furniture — must age gracefully outdoors, a
    materials-science constraint most beginners underestimate. And the fourth dimension,
    <strong>time</strong>, is designed explicitly: a newly planted landscape is expected to look
    provisional, a meadow's second summer to differ from its first, and a tree's real
    contribution to arrive fifteen years after the ribbon-cutting. Designers plant in
    succession, with fast-growing "filler" material planned for later removal, and they write
    maintenance into the design rather than leaving it as an afterthought. The studio's final
    phases — construction drawings and specifications (the legal documents that hand the idea to
    builders), cost estimating, and post-occupancy evaluation — convert the drawing into
    something a contractor can build and a client can maintain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two skills from design development deserve their own worked passages, because they are the
    ones exams and job sites test. The first is <strong>grading arithmetic</strong>. The designer
    speaks in contour lines and spot elevations, and every path, terrace and lawn is a drainage
    device. A walkway must cross 0.45 m of fall over 15 m; a 5 per cent slope (the comfortable
    maximum for an accessible path) drops 5 m per 100 m — 0.75 m over 15 m — which is more than
    the site offers. So the designer either lengthens the path (switchbacks and ramps buy
    gradient with distance), terraces it with retaining walls, or regrades the adjacent bed.
    Conversely, a plaza that must drain <em>to</em> a rain garden is drafted with a minimum
    1–2 per cent fall toward the inlet — too flat and water ponds; too steep and it feels like a
    ramp. The calculation is elementary; the judgement — where to spend fall, where to buy it
    back, where walls earn their cost — is landscape architecture.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second is <strong>planting design as composition over time</strong>. The palette is
    assembled in functional and visual layers — canopy for structure and summer shade,
    understory trees for mid-scale, shrubs for enclosure, perennials and grasses for seasonal
    rhythm, groundcover for the weed-suppressing carpet — and every plant is placed by mature
    size, which obliges the designer to draw the garden that will exist in year ten, not the one
    at installation. Practically this means spacing that looks gappy on day one (hence the
    temporary "filler" plantings scheduled for removal), irrigation zones matched to plants'
    water needs rather than to the plan's geometry, soil preparation specified like any other
    subgrade, and a maintenance regime written into the documents — because a meadow unmowed at
    the right moment becomes a thicket, and a hedge unpruned becomes a monolith. The best
    planting plans are, in effect, a budget of future labour; the worst are a first-summer
    photograph.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a flat site is a good site — grading is just flattening for
  convenience." Level ground is the hardest ground to drain. Water needs slope to move, so a
  truly flat plaza either ponds or needs engineered drainage everywhere, while the gently
  regraded landscape — 1–2 per cent away from buildings, toward an inlet or a rain garden —
  drains itself quietly for free. Grading is not cleanup after design; it <em>is</em> design,
  done in metres of rise and fall.</p>

  <h2>History of landscape design: the canon every studio argues with</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline's design vocabulary is inherited, and the canon is worth knowing because
    every contemporary project implicitly takes a position in it. The <strong>formal
    tradition</strong> — Persian paradise gardens, Italian Renaissance terraces, and its
    apotheosis in French baroque: André Le Nôtre's Versailles (from the 1660s) — imposed
    geometry on land: axes, parterres, fountains, clipped hedging, the landscape as an
    expression of order and dominion.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>English landscape garden</strong> of the eighteenth century (William Kent,
    Lancelot "Capability" Brown, Humphry Repton) rejected the geometric in favour of the
    apparently natural: rolling lawns, serpentine lakes, clumps and belts of trees, and the
    <em>ha-ha</em> — the sunken wall that kept livestock out without a visible fence. This was
    designed scenery that looked like untouched countryside, and it became the dominant park
    aesthetic exported worldwide. Running in parallel for centuries, the <strong>Japanese garden
    tradition</strong> contributed an opposite grammar: asymmetry, compression, borrowed scenery
    (<em>shakkei</em>) and symbolic abstraction — rocks as mountains, raked gravel as water — a
    vocabulary that later shaped modernism's minimalism.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>nineteenth-century public park</strong> turned the private estate idiom into
    democratic infrastructure. Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux's Greensward plan won the
    Central Park competition in 1858 and argued, with lasting influence, that scenery in cities
    is a public-health instrument — the founding act of landscape architecture as a modern
    profession. The <strong>modern movement</strong> of the mid-twentieth century (Thomas
    Church's California gardens, Garrett Eckbo, Dan Kiley) stripped ornament and composed
    gardens as <em>outdoor rooms</em> for modern living.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the <strong>ecological turn</strong> — McHarg's <em>Design with Nature</em> (1969)
    foremost — redefined the designer's brief from scenery to system: read the natural
    processes, design with them, measure success in function as well as beauty. Contemporary
    practice, from stormwater parks to landscape urbanism, is that turn's ongoing argument with
    itself, argued by critics such as Elizabeth Meyer.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "design history is a museum — old styles are obsolete, so
  the canon can be skipped." The canon is less a wardrobe of styles than a set of arguments
  still in progress. When a contemporary designer chooses an axial entry sequence, a naturalistic
  meadow or a raked-gravel court, they are answering Versailles, Capability Brown or a Kyoto
  temple garden whether they name them or not. Clients and critics will locate a project in the
  canon anyway; the trained designer is the one who knows which position they are taking.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A survey note reads: "north-west corner, standing water for two days after heavy rain." Is that inventory or analysis? Write the sentence that would make it analysis.</li>
    <li>Why do studio projects begin with bubble diagrams rather than plans?</li>
    <li>A garden path must climb 0.9 m over a run of 18 m. Can it be built as a single straight ramp at the 5 per cent comfortable maximum, without terracing?</li>
    <li>Name one idea the English landscape garden contributed and one the Japanese tradition contributed — and one place each shows up in contemporary work.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It is inventory — a recorded fact. The analysis version adds
    the consequence: "therefore grade the corner toward a rain garden and plant it with
    moisture-tolerant species." (2) Because relationships, not geometry, are what a concept must
    get right first — and a moved circle costs seconds while a moved wall costs everything. The
    bubbles also make the parti visible. (3) Yes, exactly: 0.9 ÷ 18 = 0.05, precisely 5 per cent.
    But notice the judgement question the numbers hide — a path running for 18 m at the very
    maximum feels relentless, so a good designer would consider one landing or a gentler
    average with a steeper short section. (4) English: the apparently natural landscape — rolling
    lawn, serpentine lake — which is the ancestor of every public park and suburban garden. And
    Japanese: asymmetry, compression and borrowed scenery, visible in minimalist courtyards and
    any carefully framed distant view. If you can name the ancestor, you can see the position
    any new project takes.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Landscape's medium is alive, so the process is site-first: research, analyse, concept, develop, document — then steward for years.</li>
    <li>Inventory is facts; analysis is design consequences. Good analysis usually contains the concept.</li>
    <li>Studio tools in order: programming, functional (bubble) diagrams, the parti, then design development — because moving a bubble is cheap and moving a wall is not.</li>
    <li>Grading arithmetic (per cent fall, cut-and-fill) and layered planting by mature size are the two tested working skills.</li>
    <li>Time is the fourth design dimension: fillers, succession and a written maintenance regime are part of the plan, not afterthoughts.</li>
    <li>The canon — formal, English, Japanese, public park, modern, ecological — is a set of live arguments every new project takes a position in.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You now hold the method; the next lessons supply the substance. Planting design and ecology
    take the living layers introduced here and treat them properly — species, soils, habitat.
    Landscape construction and grading takes the grading arithmetic and turns it into walls,
    paving and drainage detail. Later still: the urban scale, landscape planning, and the
    professional practice that turns a parti into a built and maintained landscape.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper currents define current practice. <strong>Scale</strong>: the same process runs
    from a residential garden to a city's open-space framework, but at metropolitan scale the
    tools shift to GIS-based suitability modelling, green-infrastructure planning and policy —
    the discipline's quiet expansion from making places to governing them. <strong>Evidence and
    performance</strong>: contemporary landscapes are expected to deliver measured outcomes —
    litres of stormwater retained per storm, degrees of urban-heat reduction under canopy,
    biodiversity indices, documented effects on wellbeing — turning post-occupancy evaluation
    from courtesy into discipline, and planting palettes into hydrological and ecological
    instruments (native and climate-adapted species, layered habitat structure, reduced
    irrigation demand). <strong>Adaptation</strong>: climate change is rewriting the base data —
    hardiness zones shifting, storm intensities rising, drought regimes hardening — so the
    field's site-first method is being applied at the scale of decades: designing for the tree
    that will mature in a hotter city, the drainage that must take tomorrow's cloudburst.
  </p>
  <p>
    The studio critic's oldest question — "what is your organising idea, and does the site agree
    with it?" — survives every technology layer, from tracing paper to parametric modelling,
    because it is the discipline itself: read the land, propose a relationship between it and
    human life, and take responsibility for the decades the answer will take to grow.
  </p>

